WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.823
When you see this,

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.280
you don't imagine that
you're hearing this.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.021
[wet punching]

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:13.163
Or when this dragonfly
floats by on the big screen,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.591
you aren't imagining
this behind the scenes.

00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:19.535
[buzzing]

00:00:19.535 --> 00:00:23.969
Foley art is one of those
unseen jobs in Hollywood,

00:00:23.969 --> 00:00:25.093
[slopping]

00:00:25.093 --> 00:00:29.200
making our experience at the
movies feel real and immersive.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.250
Take a look at 10 of
the most surprising ways

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.740
Foley artists have created sounds.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.981
[wet crunching]

00:00:39.688 --> 00:00:41.910
You can't exactly punch a real person,

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.057
so Foley artist Shaun Brennan

00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:47.170
will start by punching his bare hands.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:50.070
Punches involve a lot of sound layering.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.850
He will add gloves to beef it up,

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.730
and then take it a step further

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.150
by punching an old rolled-up phone book

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.722
wrapped in duct tape.

00:00:58.722 --> 00:01:00.238
[thudding]

00:01:00.238 --> 00:01:02.920
For a more violent and gory fight scene,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.400
he needed something more exaggerated

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.770
and found that punching
raw meat worked well.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.133
Shaun needed a cut that best imitated

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.127
the texture of a human cheek

00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:16.770
and could also lie
perfectly flat on his palm.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:20.527
The answer? A 1-pound
piece of raw flank steak.

00:01:20.527 --> 00:01:21.568
[wet punching]

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.870
Foley artists often need to cover animals

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.560
as well as humans.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.330
One of the most common animals

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.550
that they encounter is horses.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.050
Thanks to "Monty Python
and the Holy Grail,"

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.080
coconut shells have
become closely associated

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.370
with horse galloping.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:41.451
King Arthur: Whoa, there!

00:01:41.451 --> 00:01:42.826
[coconuts clacking]

00:01:42.826 --> 00:01:45.110
Narrator: Foley artist Marko Costanzo

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.080
might pull out a pair of
plastic coconut shells,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.320
but they aren't always effective.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.870
For instance, the sound
of the shells comes out

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:54.650
if you're doing the sounds for a horse

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.220
running on cobblestone.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.010
But for a movie like "True Grit,"

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.760
where the horses mostly
run on dirt and grass,

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.530
the sound of the shells
wouldn't resonate at all.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.930
The low-end thud most
associated with horse hooves

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.329
would come out better
with a pair of rocks.

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:10.462
[thudding]

00:02:10.462 --> 00:02:13.640
When a horse is moving, you
won't just hear the hooves,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.840
so you'll have to layer in
a few other sounds as well.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.750
If you need some tail flicks in there,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.950
waving around shredded
rope does a good imitation

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.372
of a horse's tail.

00:02:23.372 --> 00:02:24.588
[flicking]

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.860
When creating the sound
of an animal eating,

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.730
the first thing you need to figure out

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.780
is if the food is wet or dry.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.630
Making the sound of a horse eating

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:41.020
is also a particular challenge.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.870
They typically eat dry
food, which is easy.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.942
They also have snouts, however,

00:02:45.942 --> 00:02:48.550
which can complicate
how the sound gets made.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.440
To account for the snout,
Marko makes a cavern

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.330
by cupping his hands over
his mouth while chewing.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.673
This creates a cavity of sound.

00:02:56.673 --> 00:02:58.273
[crunching]

00:02:58.273 --> 00:03:00.950
For another take of a
scene, he had to pretend

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.380
to be a horse eating out of a bucket.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.890
Now, he couldn't actually
stick his head into a bucket.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.187
He found that chewing

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:10.760
while putting his head
between the legs of a stool

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.743
created the exact echo
he was searching for.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.050
Foley artist Matt Davies

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.500
has worked on several horror films.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.080
A few of them called for the
sounds of falling intestines,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.250
so he successfully
experimented with raw chicken.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.980
Not only does the chicken's texture

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.696
give just the right sound,

00:03:32.696 --> 00:03:34.421
but the more you handle this meat,

00:03:34.421 --> 00:03:36.840
the warmer and slimier it gets,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.250
making it perfect for multiple takes.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.810
[squelching]

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.810
But raw chicken isn't
always the cleanest product

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.640
for Foley artists to utilize.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.390
So when working on the
horror movie "V/H/S/2,"

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.430
he found he could get the same effect

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.160
squeezing and squishing chocolate pudding

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.033
and latex hoses for intestinal movement.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.510
Remember this dragonfly from
the opening of "Men in Black"?

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.010
The camera gets up close to the insect

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.920
and follows it around, so
the viewer needs to be able

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.763
to hear all of the buzzing.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.660
When Marko was tasked with
recreating this sound,

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.400
he first thought about playing around

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.100
with different types of paper.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.620
He tried putting a card in a bicycle spoke

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.300
and then spinning the wheel around.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.360
However, it was mostly the
sound of the metal spoke

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:26.400
that came through.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.090
The dragonfly needed to sound organic.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:31.300
He eventually found that what worked best

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.110
was a handheld fan.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.640
With this device, he
could more easily account

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.720
for the insect's variation in movement

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.868
by sticking his finger on the blade.

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:41.826
[whirring]

00:04:41.826 --> 00:04:44.987
Plus, there was no metal aftereffect.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:52.440
Shaun Brennan works on
a lot of action movies,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.495
which means he's needed
to create the sounds

00:04:54.495 --> 00:04:57.650
of a gnarly head stomp and smash.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:00.810
For a head crack, you need
a bone-crunching effect

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.443
to get the sound of a skull being crushed.

00:05:03.443 --> 00:05:04.884
[crunching]

00:05:04.884 --> 00:05:08.900
Ideally the object will
provide a crunch and a smush.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:12.090
While celery is usually
great for a bone crack,

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.671
it sounds too dry.

00:05:13.671 --> 00:05:14.504
[cracking]

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.090
Shaun said that on one occasion,

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.340
they had just stocked the
kitchen with apples for snacking

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.030
when a Foley mixer
suggested trying one out.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.080
So he took a few pieces of the fruit

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.941
and stomped on them with his foot.

00:05:27.941 --> 00:05:29.730
[wet crunching]

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.680
The apple not only cracks
when you stomp on it,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:35.260
but it also gives you
that squishy element,

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:39.002
which you wouldn't get from
drier fruit or veggies.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.540
Many swords in movies have
a ringing quality to them,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:50.100
but it is difficult to make
this sound with a real sword,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.660
because not only would
you get the ringing sound,

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:56.640
but you would also get an
unpleasant scraping noise.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.510
So how do you get the
ringing without the scraping?

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.901
For starters, Matt Davies knew

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:05.851
he still needed to use a metal object,

00:06:05.851 --> 00:06:08.530
and he landed on a metal shelf support.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:12.210
However, it couldn't make
the desired sound on its own,

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.290
so he took a cello bow and
bowed the side of the metal.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.830
Then he immediately waved
it around in the air.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:23.755
You can hear what Matt describes
as a "magical quality."

00:06:23.755 --> 00:06:25.130
[ringing]

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.430
Foley artists Dawn Lunsford
and Alicia Stevenson

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.730
worked on "Bumblebee" and
created many of the sounds

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.233
of the adorable "Transformers" character.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.880
While the sounds of transforming
between car and autobot

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.300
were done digitally, the
character still moved around

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.260
and interacted with the world a lot,

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.010
which required some clever Foley work.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.410
For the shot where
Bumblebee sits on a couch

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.950
and then breaks it, they
had to get both the sound

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.530
of the massive creature
squashing the couch

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.000
and the couch itself collapsing.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.790
To get an object both heavy enough

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:08.500
to capture Bumblebee's size
as well as his metal features,

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:12.110
Alicia banged an old lawn
mower against a car door.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:13.890
Then, on a separate track,

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:17.130
Dawn hit a car seat
cushion with her hands.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:18.247
[rustling]

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.410
Sometimes Marko needs to mimic the vocals

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.840
of certain animals when a digital library

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.210
just doesn't cut it.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.375
Usually he will use his mouth.

00:07:30.375 --> 00:07:32.375
[whimpers]

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:37.410
But for a mouse squeaking
and a small bird chirping,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.683
he found that it was better

00:07:38.683 --> 00:07:41.027
to let some objects do the talking.

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:42.502
[squeaking]

00:07:42.502 --> 00:07:44.630
So he used this wooden chair leg.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.070
Marko found that if he took the wooden leg

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.170
and attached a squeaky knob to it,

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.280
he could create the sounds
of both a bird and a mouse

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:53.570
by just turning it.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:57.020
He will do fast and small
turns for the mouse,

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.581
and for the bird, each turn
will go on a little longer.

00:08:00.581 --> 00:08:01.506
[squeaking]

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:07.378
When Matt had to work on something

00:08:07.378 --> 00:08:09.586
like the zombies in "V/H/S/2,"

00:08:09.586 --> 00:08:11.794
it wasn't the fleshy chewing sounds

00:08:11.794 --> 00:08:13.016
that were the problem.

00:08:13.016 --> 00:08:14.516
[snarling and chewing]

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.050
The real challenge came when imitating

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:19.670
these creatures vocally.

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:23.270
There's a huge history of
zombie noises to draw on.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.800
However, Matt has found that

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.000
some of the classic zombie sounds

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.163
just consist of a lot of grunting,

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:33.240
which feels less scary today.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.560
He found a lot of sounds in
more modern zombie features

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.267
come closer to how gory their
noises would be in reality.

00:08:40.267 --> 00:08:42.192
[snarling]

00:08:42.192 --> 00:08:46.160
So he decided to make the sounds
more guttural and phlegmy,

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:49.743
and he found the result
to be much scarier.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:56.940
Ian Phillips: Thanks for watching.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.430
If you like learning about how
sounds are made for movies,

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.870
subscribe for many more
videos just like this one.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:04.370
And let us know what other Foley sounds

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:07.283
you'd like to see in
action in the comments.

